#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ab388a-b152-58aa-9764-ba4f8cabdf11" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "4d69af5a-1ec2-53b7-ad94-b292c6beaf47" :question/math-academy-id "e-1502" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n90, \\: -30, \\: 10, \\: -\\dfrac{10}{3}, \\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{(-30)}{90} = -\\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5580d5-b666-5e95-b841-1813c1a71b84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term"}] :question/id #uuid "5d76d255-8f67-5f95-b789-b4840469dfc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1504" :question/problem "Find the $7$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n1, \\: 2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/65/653f9ccfe28f5e3630afd30562064f1d790245b227896ea7db2da4265f7bac1a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{2}{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $2.$\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $7$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/6a/6ae31d38f90d943b5bd802652d0dedd4925351b1ffadd6398d018a1dd4c7f0d3.png)\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is $64.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434acf34-51e5-5733-a50d-a5199def8c77" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "f25cd97e-0a26-5b3b-a4f7-aef8acb561a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1505" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{16}{8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(-\\frac{1}{3})} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{27})}{(\\frac{1}{9})} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20303666-34cb-53cc-9d00-2f472e0bb315" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Using the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "ad13200a-0939-5b3e-a678-7db922cf0b5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1796" :question/problem "Given that $x$, $3x$, $36$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=x,\\,{a}_{2}=3x,\\,{a}_{3}=36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{36}{3x}=\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{12}{x} \\\\ 3x & =12 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20303666-34cb-53cc-9d00-2f472e0bb315" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Using the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55959341-9939-5fcb-a730-41d470011ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520124} {:answer/id #uuid "a778ef61-0261-5eec-98a3-a7479f594baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520221} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b6755c-a6b5-59ab-9d2b-90b6f3c5976c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520260} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe2db88-963e-57e9-9c11-2568e077eafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520307} {:answer/id #uuid "e605dc1e-a226-53f6-8081-124213074e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a778ef61-0261-5eec-98a3-a7479f594baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520221} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebff470-79e5-5ba7-b4ee-54414e7698e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aefd99db-cf1d-5552-bc9d-e8816ab40f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-101241" :question/problem "Given that $50$, $5x$, and $12.5x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=50,\\,{a}_{2}=5x,\\,{a}_{3}=12.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5x}{50}=\\frac{x}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12.5x}{5x}=2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5 & =\\frac{x}{10} \\\\ 25 & =x \\\\ x & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078546 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20303666-34cb-53cc-9d00-2f472e0bb315" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Using the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1582d224-094e-567c-a4d0-6a766eb83c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520020} {:answer/id #uuid "3acc9f02-23f0-5267-8e8f-2d43767b5290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520073} {:answer/id #uuid "75fba1b8-0f25-52b0-a4e5-d85355af26f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520162} {:answer/id #uuid "8082c854-a0c3-5658-9917-19fdd468998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520176} {:answer/id #uuid "e86e41a3-1743-5eae-a35d-1b9bb6aa8392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1582d224-094e-567c-a4d0-6a766eb83c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520020} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd92fce-27a7-528b-8997-468fa342ecb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e89be9b-05fd-570e-aab3-708af6042f1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109799" :question/problem "Given that $8$, $6x$, and $10x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=8,\\,{a}_{2}=6x,\\,{a}_{3}=10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{8}=\\frac{3x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{10x}{6x}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{4} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{20}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ab388a-b152-58aa-9764-ba4f8cabdf11" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9eb9c31-94f2-588f-821e-14ddefff02d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9eb9c31-94f2-588f-821e-14ddefff02d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520336} :answer-field/id #uuid "510f525c-3dca-5fa8-8675-d8d314365702" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfa27842-a6ff-52d4-867b-7142afc0e5fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-250632" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n25,\\,-5,\\,1,\\,-\\frac{1}{5},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of this geometric sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-5)}{25}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078544 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06ab388a-b152-58aa-9764-ba4f8cabdf11" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94638c3-3706-5557-8410-7e36882e8a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94638c3-3706-5557-8410-7e36882e8a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520222} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a2f5ca-3647-52a4-8e96-552ff18e0cf0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d70384a-18d5-5ae8-aba3-5e0332c40c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-250656" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n243,\\,-162,\\,108,\\,-72,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of this geometric sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence is the constant we multiply by to get to the next term. In other words, it is the ratio of consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio for our sequence is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-162)}{243}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5580d5-b666-5e95-b841-1813c1a71b84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bca39d98-6ddb-59e1-9d17-5b686f32e444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca39d98-6ddb-59e1-9d17-5b686f32e444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520256} :answer-field/id #uuid "a292ed97-b950-5627-8916-577ea6d8682b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feb40554-9f62-5369-873c-1a4212bcc47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-250679" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,10,\\,50,\\,250,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe sixth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/64/64ab493abc4e32afc36676b20cf0e8898c4d0ba07c086280e426890c7bdfb70a.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{10}{2}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $5.$ \n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $6$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/32/32f60b73a942c4e792f3629b5f4824f1c639a25e03491a36f81679d126bcd685.png)\n\nTherefore, the $6$th term is $6250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078545 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c5580d5-b666-5e95-b841-1813c1a71b84" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceff70a8-de66-5f92-b01d-f66ba9169bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077241} {:answer/id #uuid "f1332d1f-d414-5f55-be3e-9c7ab440b5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077242} {:answer/id #uuid "f4feeef8-3156-5c08-8feb-eb7d0a1dd4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077243} {:answer/id #uuid "4920fd01-08c5-54d9-b99e-829f9f4be665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077244} {:answer/id #uuid "650c0a51-6831-56e7-8bb6-5c93983840ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1332d1f-d414-5f55-be3e-9c7ab440b5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077242} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a14d464-fffd-5524-9ec5-b14f163649ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40cd52fc-c3d5-5074-bd5e-a3c26e4deaea" :question/math-academy-id "q-5241" :question/problem "Find the $5$th term of the following geometric sequence.\n\n$$\n2,\\,-4,\\,8,\\,-16,\\,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7c6697d2a70a7499e6a834f3e588257c88f5da433c7365b9b1777314377e122d.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $-2.$\n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $5$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/a5/a5cd75c3e262f76d12da48cd8e86bf04b2532f7e7084d1968ac30bd103373f64.png)\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434acf34-51e5-5733-a50d-a5199def8c77" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42dcbbf9-138e-544e-83b5-ce1af281813a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520086} {:answer/id #uuid "b5134393-8754-56f1-8c80-963cf9ae8285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520245} {:answer/id #uuid "c8637f9e-b6c7-563c-8fbb-5358af318a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520272} {:answer/id #uuid "d89c9e75-1551-5bde-9f68-e97033024c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520296} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ee9576-7d5a-5cf5-bd8b-7b448a345983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5134393-8754-56f1-8c80-963cf9ae8285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520245} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd1d0cb-5c67-54b2-9a8e-55d3e3199e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bf4971e-04f4-56ea-b8f7-ec174707b886" :question/math-academy-id "q-52474" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-1,\\,-6,\\,-36,\\,…$\n\nII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$\n\nIII. $2,\\,7,\\,12,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-6)}{(-1)}=6 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-36)}{(-6)}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{12}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434acf34-51e5-5733-a50d-a5199def8c77" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Geometric Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4119ef5a-ce8f-557a-86d3-930060c2d7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520083} {:answer/id #uuid "466b273e-c846-58e4-b675-7fc925b9a67b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520096} {:answer/id #uuid "d2daa668-266e-57f8-9b6d-9aba42612c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520287} {:answer/id #uuid "d499038b-1ca3-508e-ade3-a293c9a995d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520291} {:answer/id #uuid "dd63c68f-2391-56cd-bd26-5b2e6bf05ebe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4119ef5a-ce8f-557a-86d3-930060c2d7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520083} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cef64b1-c161-50e4-82e1-fafe2dafb2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "011f3c60-0a40-5c48-8e42-7ae45f302869" :question/math-academy-id "q-79509" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $120,\\,80,\\,30,\\,…$\n\nII. $-243,\\,27,\\,-3,\\,…$\n\nIII. $225,\\,45,\\,9,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{80}{120}=\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{30}{80}=\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{27}{(-243)}=-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-3)}{27}=-\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is also geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{45}{225}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{9}{45}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences II and III could be geometric sequences."}]}
